'''Lake Chelan''' ({{IPAc-en|ʃ|ə|ˈ|l|æ|n}} {{respell|shə|LAN|'}}) is a narrow, {{convert|50.5|mi|abbr=on}} long [[lake]] in [[Chelan County]], north-central Washington state, U.S.<ref name=EPA_1994 /> It is an [[overdeepened]] lake and resembles a [[fjord]], with an average width of {{convert|1.3|mi|km|abbr=on}}. Near its upper end, the lake surface lies more than {{convert|6,600|ft|m|abbr=on}} below peaks less than {{convert|3|mi|km|abbr=on}} away.<ref name="Whetten1967a"/><ref name="Lillquist2016a"/> Before 1927, Lake Chelan was [[List of lakes of Washington (state)|the largest natural lake in the state]], in terms of both surface area and water volume.<ref name=Ecology/><ref name= BortlesonOthers1976a>{{cite web|url=https://apps.ecology.wa.gov/publications/SummaryPages/WSB43e.html|last1=Bortleson|first1=G.C.|last2=Dion|first2=N.P.|last3= McConnell| first3=J.B.| last4=Nelson| first4=L.M.| title= Reconnaissance data on lakes in Washington -- Vol. 5, Chelan, Ferry, Kittitas, Klickitat, Okanogan, and Yakima Counties| publisher=Washington State Department of Ecology| date=1976| accessdate=2023-02-03}}</ref> Upon the completion of [[Lake Chelan Dam]] in 1927, the elevation of the lake was increased by {{convert|21|ft|m|abbr=on}} to its present maximum-capacity elevation of {{convert|1100|ft|m|abbr=on}}.<ref name=Ecology/>

With a maximum depth of {{convert|1,486|ft|m||abbr=on}}, Lake Chelan is the third deepest lake in the United States behind [[Crater Lake]], the deepest, and [[Lake Tahoe]], the second deepest. Because of overdeepening, the sides of this lake drop steeply to its bottom. The deepest part of Lake Chelan lies as much as {{convert|436|ft|m|abbr=on}} below sea level. In places, the bedrock floor of the valley occupied by Lake Chelan, which is buried by [[Pleistocene]] [[glacial]] and [[lacustrine]] [[sediment]]s, lies at least {{convert|1529|ft|m|abbr=on}} below [[sea level]]. Two communities lie on the southern end of the lake, and a third sits at the far north end, providing a gateway to [[North Cascades National Park]].<ref name="Whetten1967a">{{cite journal |last1= Whetten |first1=J.T. |year = 1967 |title = Lake Chelan Washington: bottom and sub-bottom topography |journal = Limnology and Oceanography |volume = 12 |issue = 2 |pages = 253–259 |doi = 10.4319/lo.1967.12.2.0253|pmid = |doi-access = free }}</ref><ref name="Lillquist2016a">{{cite book |last1= Lillquis |first1= Karl |title= Moses Coulee to Chelan Field Trip, 12 June 2016|publisher= Ellensburg Chapter, Ice Age Floods Institute |year=2016 |location= Ellensburg, Washington |page=39 |isbn= }}</ref>

==Hydrology==
On an annual basis, an average of {{convert|2200|cuft/s}} flow into the lake.<ref name=watershed>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/95161.pdf#page=4| title=Chelan Watershed Initial Assessment| date=May 1985| publisher=Washington Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-13}}</ref> Approximately 75% of the water that flows into the lake comes from two tributaries.<ref name=BioAnalysts>{{cite web|url=https://www.chelanpud.org/relicense/study/reports/3809_1.pdf#page=4| title=Historical Occurrences of Anadromous Salmonids in Lake Chelan, Washington| last1=Hillman | first1=T.W.| last2=Giorgi| first2=A.E.| date=February 2000| website=Chelan Public Utilities District| publisher=BioAnalysts, Inc.| accessdate=2015-01-17}}</ref> The [[Stehekin River]] alone contributes 65% of all water to Lake Chelan, averaging {{convert|1401|cuft/s|abbr=on}} annually.<ref name=ddt>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/0610022.pdf#page=21| title=Lake Chelan Watershed DDT and PCB Total Maximum Daily Load: Water Quality Improvement Report| last1=Coots| first1=Randy| date=June 2006| publisher=Washington State Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-19}}</ref> The other major tributary, [[Railroad Creek]], averages {{convert|202|cuft/s|abbr=on}} annually.<ref name=watershed /> The remaining water is added via a number of smaller tributaries as well as direct rain and snowfall.

With a maximum depth of {{convert|1486|ft|m}},<ref name=watershed /> Lake Chelan is the [[List of lakes by depth|third deepest lake]] in the United States, and the [[List of lakes by depth|25th deepest in the world]]. At its deepest, the lake bottom is {{convert|388|ft|m}} below sea level.<ref name=Wapato>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/1103049.pdf#page=11| title=Lake Chelan Wapato Basin Total Phosphorus Total Maximum Daily Load| date=November 2011| publisher=State of Washington Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-18}}</ref> The total watershed of the lake is {{convert|924|sqmi|sqkm}}<ref name=BioAnalysts /><ref>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/95161.pdf#page=2| title=Chelan Watershed Initial Assessment| date=May 1985| publisher=Washington Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-13}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ecy.wa.gov/programs/wq/tmdl/LkChelanTMDLSummary.html| title=Water Quality Improvement Project: Lake Chelan Area: Multi-Parameter| date=March 2013| publisher=Department of Ecology - State of Washington| accessdate=2015-01-17}}</ref> More than 90% of the watershed is forested land. The remainder of the basin is composed of the lake itself (5.6%) and agriculture (3.5%).<ref name=program>{{cite web| url=http://fixthatleak.org/mitigation/docs/Chelan_prospectus.pdf#page=23| title=Draft In-Leau Fee Program Prospectus and Compensation Planning Framework for Lower Lake Chelan| date=June 2010| publisher=The Watershed Company| accessdate=2015-01-19| archive-date=2015-09-09| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150909012855/http://fixthatleak.org/mitigation/docs/Chelan_prospectus.pdf#page=23| url-status=dead}}</ref>

==Geology==
The fjord-like topography of the Lake Chelan valley results from repeated glacial erosion and deposition (maybe nine or ten times) during the Pleistocene Period.<ref name="Lillquist2016a"/><ref name="Freeman1944a">{{cite journal |last1= Freeman |first1=O.W. |year = 1944 |title = Glaciation and some human relationships at Lake Chelan |journal = Northwest Science |volume = 18 |issue = 3 |pages = 59–62 |doi = |pmid = }}</ref> The last episode of glacial erosion and deposition in the basin occurred during the [[Last Glacial Maximum]] about 21,000 years ago. At that time, in the upper [[Similkameen River]] valley of British Columbia, the Skagit Lobe split from the Okanogan Lobe of the [[Cordilleran ice sheet]] and advanced south into the [[Skagit River]] drainage.<ref name=" Riedel2017a">{{cite journal |last1= Riedel |first1=J.L. |year = 2017 |title = Deglaciation of the North Cascade Range, Washington and British Columbia, from the last glacial maximum to the Holocene |journal = Cuadernos de investigación geográfica/Geographical Research Letters |volume = 43 |issue = |pages = 59–62 |doi = |pmid = }}</ref> Skagit ice passed through Fisher and Rainy passes, and down Bridge Creek into the Lake Chelan valley. The glacial lobe flowed down the Lake Chelan valley until meeting glacial ice of the main Okanogan Lobe advancing up the valley from the [[Columbia River]] drainage near [[Manson, Washington|Manson]]. The deposits of the northwestward advancing Okanagan lobe are characterized by large, [[basalt]] [[glacial erratic]]s.<ref name="Lillquist2016a"/> As the Skagit Lobe during the Last Glacial Maximum and glacial lobes during older glaciations flowed to the southeast down the Lake Chelan valley, they excavated the deep glacial trough that is now occupied by Lake Chelan.<ref name=" Riedel2017a"/> The depth of the Lucerne Basin and the elevation of glacial [[till]] and [[moraine]]s and glacier-scoured bedrock on the walls of the overdeepened Lake Chelan valley indicates that the thickness of the Skagit Lobe was over {{convert|1|mi|km|abbr=on}}.<ref name="Whetten1967a"/><ref name="Lillquist2016a"/>

===Basins===
Lake Chelan is composed of two basins. The lower basin, Wapato, is shallower and approximately a fourth the total length of the lake. The upper basin, Lucerne, is much deeper and extends for the remainder of the length of the lake. The two basins are separated by a sill rising to within {{convert|122|ft|abbr=on}} of the surface, at a point known as the narrows, at which the lake is only {{convert|0.35|mi|km}} wide.<ref name=Wapato />

The lower basin, Wapato, reaches a maximum depth of only {{convert|400|ft|m}}. About {{convert|600|ft|m}} of glacial sediment and rockslide deposits rest on top of the bedrock.<ref name=basins>{{cite web|url=http://www.fs.usda.gov/Internet/FSE_DOCUMENTS/stelprdb5407534.pdf|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150129153713/http://www.fs.usda.gov/Internet/FSE_DOCUMENTS/stelprdb5407534.pdf|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 29, 2015| title=Discover Lake Chelan| publisher=National Park Service| accessdate=2015-01-14}}</ref> This section of the lake is {{convert|12|mi|km}} long, and has an average depth of {{convert|190|ft|m}}.<ref name=project />  Due to the relatively modest size of this basin, water resides in this basin for only 0.8 years, compared to 10 for Lucerne Basin.<ref name=quality>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/89e36.pdf#page=14| title=Lake Chelan Water Quality Assessment| last1=Pelletier| first1=Greg| last2=Owes| first2=Harper| date=January 1989| publisher=Washington State Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-19}}</ref>  The upper Lucerne basin is {{convert|38|mi|km}} long with an average depth of {{convert|1148|ft|m}} and thus by far the larger of the two basins.<ref name=project>{{cite web|url=https://fortress.wa.gov/ecy/publications/publications/85e09.pdf#page=3| title=Lake Chelan Project Proposal| last1=Chamberlain| first1=William| last2=Singleton| first2=Lynn| last3=Jay| first3=Joe| date=1985-02-20| publisher=Washington State Department of Ecology| accessdate=2015-01-19}}</ref> It is in this part of the lake that the maximum depth of {{convert|1486|ft|m}} is found. Lucerne basin contains 92% of the water in Lake Chelan and 74% of the surface area, leaving Wapato with only 8% of the total volume of water and 26% of the surface area.<ref name=ddt /><ref name=Wapato /><ref name=basins /> The upper basin of Lake Chelan is surrounded by more mountainous terrain, resulting in few beaches along the shoreline.  Approximately {{convert|50|mi|km}} of the shoreline of this basin are in National Forest lands, and {{convert|12|mi|km}} in National Park lands.<ref name=ddt />

==Natural history==
===Climate===
The climate of Lake Chelan's watershed is varied. From the southern end of the lake in the [[rain shadow]] of the [[Cascade Range]], to the northern tip of the lake located in the eastern Cascades, the climate of Lake Chelan's watershed is as diverse as the lake is long. The south end's weather is notably dry, with [[Chelan, Washington|Chelan]] averaging only {{convert|11.4|in|cm}} of rain per year, along with {{convert|21.8|in|cm}} of snow. Stehekin receives an average of {{convert|35.5|in|cm}} of rain per year, and {{convert|122.5|in|cm}} of snow. Other than precipitation trends, the climates are remarkably similar. Both locations average around {{convert|60|°F|°C}} for a high, and {{convert|40|°F|°C}} for a low throughout the course of the year.{{cn|date=January 2023}}
