==History==
===Etymology===
The name Chelan is a [[Salishan languages|Salish]] Indigenous word, "''Tsi - Laan''," meaning 'Deep Water'.<ref>{{cite web|title=Native American Legends |publisher=USDA Forest Service - Pacific Northwest Region |url=http://www.fs.fed.us/r6/fishing/regional/enveducation/stories/native.pdf |accessdate=2007-06-26 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20060411055827/http://www.fs.fed.us/r6/fishing/regional/enveducation/stories/native.pdf |archivedate=April 11, 2006 }}</ref>
===Cities===
Due to the isolated nature of Lake Chelan, especially at its northern reaches, there is not a large population that resides along the shore. [[Chelan, Washington|The City of Chelan]], which had 4,222 residents at the [[2020 United States Census|2020 census]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Explore Census Data |url=https://data.census.gov/cedsci/table?q=Chelan%20city,%20Washington&t=Population%20Total&tid=ACSDT5Y2010.B01003 |access-date=2022-03-23 |website=data.census.gov}}</ref> is currently the only incorporated city situated along the lake shore. The city is located at the southern terminus of the lake, adjacent to the [[Lake Chelan Dam]] and the [[Chelan River]] outflow. The [[census-designated place]] of [[Manson, Washington|Manson]], which had 1,523 residents in 2020,<ref name="Census 2010">{{cite web |title=2020 United States Census Profile: Manson CDP, Washington |url=https://data.census.gov/profile/Manson_CDP,_Washington?g=1600000US5342835 |accessdate=March 15, 2023 |publisher=U.S. Census Bureau, American Factfinder}}{{cbignore|bot=medic}}</ref> is also located at the southern end of the lake. The [[Unincorporated area|unincorporated community]] of [[Stehekin]],<ref name=inventory /> with approximately 75 residents,<ref>{{cite web| url=http://www.visitstehekin.com/| title=Visit Stehekin, Washington| author=Visit Stehekin| accessdate=2015-01-13| url-status=dead| archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150101165010/http://www.visitstehekin.com/| archivedate=2015-01-01}}</ref> is located at the northern terminus of the lake, adjacent to the [[Stehekin River]] inflow. At the mouth of the Railroad Creek sits [[Lucerne, Washington|Lucerne]], a small community of private cabins served by commercial boats.<ref name=inventory>{{cite web|url=http://www.ecy.wa.gov/programs/sea/shorelines/smp/mycomments/cashmere/inventoryAnalysisRpt.pdf#page=26| title=Shoreline Inventory| publisher=Chelan County| date=2012-04-23| accessdate=2015-01-19}}</ref> Lucerne is also the primary gateway to the community of [[Holden Village]], a Lutheran retreat center located {{convert|11|mi|km|abbr=on}} inland from the lake. With approximately 50 long-term residents, Holden includes one of the few remaining public K-12 two-room schools in the contiguous United States.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.holdenvillage.org/village-life/holden-school/ |title=Holden School |publisher=Holden Village |date= |accessdate=2022-03-17 |archive-date=2015-05-01 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150501191631/http://www.holdenvillage.org/village-life/holden-school |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Economy==

===Fishing===
Fishing is a popular recreational activity on Lake Chelan. The following fish are or were native to the lake: [[Bull Trout]], [[Westslope cutthroat trout]], [[Largescale sucker]], [[Longnose sucker]], [[Bridgelip sucker]], [[Northern pikeminnow]], [[Peamouth]], [[Redside shiner]], [[Mountain whitefish]], [[Pygmy whitefish]].<ref name=fish>{{cite web| url=http://www.fws.gov/midcolumbiariverfro/pdf/What%20Happened%20to%20Bull%20Trout%20in%20Lake%20Chelan.pdf#page=10| title=What Happened to Bull Trout in Lake Chelan? An Examination of the Historical Evidence| last1=Nelson| first1=Mark C.| date=2012-01-20| publisher=U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service| accessdate=2015-01-17| archive-date=2015-09-24| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924044700/http://www.fws.gov/midcolumbiariverfro/pdf/What%20Happened%20to%20Bull%20Trout%20in%20Lake%20Chelan.pdf#page=10| url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name=fish2>{{cite web|url=https://www.chelanpud.org/relicense/study/reports/3809_1.pdf#page=9| title=Historical Occurrences of Anadromous Salmonids in Lake Chelan, Washington| last1=Hillman | first1=T.W.| last2=Giorgi| first2=A.E.| date=February 2000| website=Chelan Public Utilities District| publisher=BioAnalysts, Inc.| accessdate=2015-01-17}}</ref>

In addition to these native species, six species have been introduced to the lake, primarily for sport fishing purposes: [[Yellowstone cutthroat trout]], [[Rainbow trout]], [[Sockeye salmon|Kokanee]], [[Brook trout]], [[Chinook salmon]], [[Lake trout]]<ref name=fish /><ref name=fish2 />

====State records====
In 2013, a {{convert|35.63|lb|kg|abbr=on}} [[Lake Trout]] was caught, setting the state record.<ref>{{cite web| url=http://wdfw.wa.gov/fishing/records/search.php?View=all| title=Freshwater and Saltwater Records| publisher=Washington Department of Fish & Wildlife| accessdate=2015-01-17| archive-date=2015-01-18| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150118085207/http://wdfw.wa.gov/fishing/records/search.php?View=all| url-status=dead}}</ref>

=== Winemaking ===
The [[Lake Chelan AVA]] surrounds the southernmost 12 miles of Lake Chelan.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Federal Register :: Request Access |url=https://unblock.federalregister.gov/ |access-date=2023-06-21 |website=unblock.federalregister.gov}}</ref> A subzone of the larger [[Columbia Valley AVA]], the Lake Chelan AVA is home to 31 tasting rooms.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-04-27 |title=Facts and Figures – Lake Chelan Wine Valley |url=http://lakechelanwinevalley.com/facts-and-figures/ |access-date=2023-06-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200427192955/http://lakechelanwinevalley.com/facts-and-figures/ |archive-date=2020-04-27 }}</ref>

==Protected lands==
At the north end of the lake, surrounding the town of Stehekin, is [[Lake Chelan National Recreation Area]] (NRA). Bordering the Lake Chelan NRA is the [[Lake Chelan-Sawtooth Wilderness]]. The [[Wenatchee National Forest]] surrounds much of the lake on either side. Two state parks are located on the southern edge, not far from the city of Chelan. These state parks are [[Twenty-Five Mile Creek State Park]] and [[Lake Chelan State Park]].<ref name=StateParks>{{cite web| url=http://www.parks.wa.gov/DocumentCenter/View/1286#page=7| title=Lake Chelan Area State Parks Management Plan| date=January 2009| publisher=Washington State Parks and Recreation Committee| accessdate=2015-01-17| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150118085207/http://www.parks.wa.gov/DocumentCenter/View/1286#page=7| archive-date=2015-01-18| url-status=dead}}</ref>

In addition to the protected land located directly on the shores of Lake Chelan, Stehekin serves not only as a gateway to the Lake Chelan NRA, but also to the rest of the [[North Cascades National Park]] Complex, [[Stephen Mather Wilderness]], and adjacent National Forest Wilderness Areas.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nps.gov/noca/planyourvisit/stehekin.htm| title=Stehekin| publisher=National Park Service| access-date=2015-01-17}}</ref> Approximately 87% of the Lake Chelan watershed is owned by either federal, state, or local entities, with the rest in private ownership.<ref name=program />

